The code statements that lead to the bug are:
ctrl = devm_spi_alloc_host(&udev->dev, sizeof(struct ch341_spi_dev));
ch341->rx_buf = devm_kzalloc(&udev->dev, ch341->rx_len, GFP_KERNEL);
ch341->tx_buf = devm_kzalloc(&udev->dev, CH341_PACKET_LENGTH, GFP_KERNEL);
Reason:
Using `&udev->dev` ties the lifetime of the SPI controller and data buffers to the parent USB device rather than the USB interface. When the driver is unbound (e.g., due to probe deferral or configuration changes), these devm-managed resources are not released because the USB device remains. This causes memory leaks and improper device tree placement.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
ctrl = devm_spi_alloc_host(&intf->dev, sizeof(struct ch341_spi_dev));
ch341->rx_buf = devm_kzalloc(&intf->dev, ch341->rx_len, GFP_KERNEL);
ch341->tx_buf = devm_kzalloc(&intf->dev, CH341_PACKET_LENGTH, GFP_KERNEL);
Reason:
Using `&intf->dev` (the USB interface device) ties the resources to the interface’s lifetime. When the driver is unbound, the interface device is released, and all devm-allocated resources are automatically freed, preventing memory leaks and ensuring correct device tree hierarchy.

